---
import PageHeader from "@/components/ui/PageHeader.astro";
import { siteConfig } from "@/config/site";
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { withBreadcrumbs } from "@/lib/schema";

const crumbs = [{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Privacy" }];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description={`What ${siteConfig.name} does with information about visitors and email subscribers: what is stored, who handles it, and how to unsubscribe.`}
  structuredData={withBreadcrumbs({ "@type": "WebPage", name: "Privacy" }, crumbs)}
>
  <PageHeader
    title="Privacy."
    lead="What this site does with information about you, as it works today."
    crumbs={crumbs}
  />

  <div class="shell">
    <div class="prose-article max-w-[37rem]">
      <h2>What the site keeps</h2>
      <p>
        There are no accounts, comments, or contact forms, and the site runs no analytics or
        advertising scripts. Its own code doesn't set cookies.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you use the switch in the header, your choice of light or dark mode is saved in your
        browser's local storage and isn't sent anywhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        A box offering the weekly map can open once per visit, after you have been on a page for a
        while. When you close it, your browser's local storage keeps a note saying so, with the
        date, so it stays away for 30 days; if you sign up, the note keeps it away for good. Your
        browser's session storage also notes that it has opened, so it doesn't open again during the
        same visit. None of this is sent anywhere, and clearing your browser's site data removes it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The only other thing kept is your email address, if you sign up for the weekly map (below).
      </p>

      <h2>Email subscriptions</h2>
      <p>
        If you sign up for the weekly map, in that box or at the end of a post, the subscriber list
        keeps:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>your email address;</li>
        <li>
          whether you are waiting to confirm, subscribed, or unsubscribed, and when each of those
          happened;
        </li>
        <li>
          two random codes for the links in your emails: one to confirm, stored only in scrambled
          (hashed) form, and one to unsubscribe.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Nothing else is stored with it: no name and no IP address. It is used only to send you the
        weekly map: one email a week with a Google Maps list of places I've found on the road.
      </p>
      <p>
        You are only subscribed once you open the link in the confirmation email and press Confirm.
        The link works for 48 hours. If you don't confirm, nothing more is sent unless the address
        is entered in the form again. Unconfirmed addresses are deleted automatically after their
        link expires, at the next sign-up by anyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every email includes a link to unsubscribe. Open it and press Unsubscribe, and no more
        emails are sent. Your address then stays on the list marked as unsubscribed, so it isn't
        emailed again by mistake. To have it deleted completely, <a href="/contact/">contact me</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Who handles it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare</a> hosts the site and
          stores the subscriber list. To deliver the pages, it handles the requests your browser
          makes, including your IP address. When you use the sign-up form, its Turnstile check looks
          at your IP address and browser to tell people from bots.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy">Resend</a> sends the confirmation email,
          and a short note to me when someone confirms, so it handles your address to deliver them.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The list is kept by {siteConfig.author.name}, who writes this journal. When you confirm,
        that note with your address arrives in my own inbox. Each service works under its own
        privacy policy.
      </p>

      <h2>Search</h2>
      <p>
        Search runs in your browser: it loads a list of posts from this site, and what you type
        isn't sent anywhere as you search.
      </p>

      <h2>Links to other sites</h2>
      <p>
        The links to Instagram, X, and Reddit, and the share buttons on posts, take you to those
        sites, which have their own privacy policies. Nothing from them loads here unless you click.
      </p>
      <p>
        The lists in the weekly email are on Google Maps. Opening or saving one happens on Google's
        side, under <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        If you have a question about any of this, <a href="/contact/">contact me</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
